{"name":"Antique Wicker Basket","hex":"#f3d3a1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"antique","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having existed in ancient times, descended from antiquity; used especially in reference to Greece and Rome.","Belonging to former times, not modern, out of date, old-fashioned."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["In general, anything very old; specifically:\n An old object perceived as having value because of its aesthetic or historical significance.","An old object perceived as having value because of its aesthetic or historical significance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/antique"},{"term":"wicker","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flexible branch or twig of a plant such as willow, used in weaving baskets and furniture.","Wickerwork."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of wickerwork."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wicker"},{"term":"basket","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lightweight woven container, generally round, open at the top, and tapering toward the bottom.","A bed for a cat."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To place in a basket or baskets.","To cross-collateralize the royalty advances for multiple works so that the creator is not paid until all of those works have achieved a certain level of success."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/basket"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wicker","blocks":[{"text":"Wicker is a method of weaving used to make products such as furniture and baskets, as well as a descriptor to classify such products. It is the oldest furniture-making method, dating as far back as c. 3000 BC. Wicker was first documented in ancient Egypt, then having been made from pliable plant material, but in modern times it is made from any pliable, easily woven material. The word wicker or \"wisker\" is believed to be of Scandinavian origin: vika, which means \"to fold\" in Swedish. Wicker is traditionally made of plant-based material, such as willow, rattan, reed, and bamboo, though the term also applies to products woven from synthetic fibers. Wicker is light yet sturdy, making it suitable for items that will be moved often like porch and patio furniture. Rushwork and wickerwork are terms used in England. A typical braiding pattern is called Wiener Geflecht, Viennese braiding, as it was invented in 18th century Vienna and later most prominently used with the Thonet coffeehouse chair."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Apples_in_basket_2018_G1.jpg/330px-Apples_in_basket_2018_G1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wicker","wikidata":"Q1081013"},{"title":"Antique","blocks":[{"text":"An antique is an item perceived as having value because of its aesthetic or historical significance, and often defined as at least 100 years old, although the term is often used loosely to describe any object that is old. An antique is usually an item that is collected or desirable because of its age, beauty, rarity, condition, utility, personal emotional connection, or other unique features. It is an object that represents a previous era or time period in human history. Vintage and collectible are used to describe items that are old, but do not meet the 100-year criterion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/06_Restoration_of_gilded_mirror_in_Muzeum_Gornoslaskie%2C_Bytom%2C_Poland_-_furniture_restorer_working.jpg/330px-06_Restoration_of_gilded_mirror_in_Muzeum_Gornoslaskie%2C_Bytom%2C_Poland_-_furniture_restorer_working.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antique","wikidata":"Q472760"},{"title":"Basket","blocks":[{"text":"A basket is a container that is traditionally constructed from stiff fibers, and can be made from a range of materials, including wood splints, runners, and cane. While most baskets are made from plant materials, other materials such as horsehair, baleen, or metal wire can be used. Baskets are generally woven by hand. Some baskets are fitted with a lid, while others are left open on top."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Baskets_in_Haikou_03.jpg/330px-Baskets_in_Haikou_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basket","wikidata":"Q201097"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Large_Oval_Storage_Basket_MET_36.3.57a-b_EGDP011891.jpg/500px-Large_Oval_Storage_Basket_MET_36.3.57a-b_EGDP011891.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Large_Oval_Storage_Basket_MET_36.3.57a-b_EGDP011891.jpg/1280px-Large_Oval_Storage_Basket_MET_36.3.57a-b_EGDP011891.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Large_Oval_Storage_Basket_MET_36.3.57a-b_EGDP011891.jpg/500px-Large_Oval_Storage_Basket_MET_36.3.57a-b_EGDP011891.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wicker basket, Egypt, New Kingdom, c. 1492–1473 BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALarge_Oval_Storage_Basket_MET_36.3.57a-b_EGDP011891.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Portable_basket_for_tea_utensils%2C_by_Hayakawa_Shokosai_I%2C_Japan%2C_Osaka%2C_1883_-_Asian_Art_Museum_of_San_Francisco_-_DSC01496.JPG/500px-Portable_basket_for_tea_utensils%2C_by_Hayakawa_Shokosai_I%2C_Japan%2C_Osaka%2C_1883_-_Asian_Art_Museum_of_San_Francisco_-_DSC01496.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Portable_basket_for_tea_utensils%2C_by_Hayakawa_Shokosai_I%2C_Japan%2C_Osaka%2C_1883_-_Asian_Art_M